+
+### ADR-026 — Domain-aware coupled solving and an explicit evaluation failure policy — Accepted
+**Incident (MI300X, 2026-10-09).** `python scripts/eval_generations.py` died in M1's `model_seen` suite with
+`ExprError: log(): math domain error`. BASE (3 suites) and M1 (claim, model) had completed and were saved.
+**Root cause (reproduced deterministically, `tests/test_solver_domain_and_failures.py`).** M1 built the model
+{colebrook, laminar_friction} for a pipe task. With Re and f_D unknown this is a 2x2 strongly-connected block, which
+`laws.solve` handed to `scipy.optimize.root(method="hybr")` from x0 = (1, 1) with an unguarded residual. Powell's
+third trial point was (f_D, Re) = (133.66, -4.197): Re < 0 violates the variable's declared bound (Re >= 0), the
+Colebrook log argument became -0.0517, the ExprError propagated through scipy, ModelEnv and the runner, and the
+process exited. The agent's action was legal; the defect was in the solver (the scalar path was already domain-aware,
+the coupled path was not) and in the runner (one episode's exception killed the whole evaluation). An exhaustive
+search on the pre-fix code (68 benchmark tasks x every pair of the 30 library fragments, 31.6k solves) found this
+combination - on all 12 pipe tasks - to be the ONLY one that raised.
+**Decision (solver).** `laws.core._solve_coupled`: stage 1 is the original Powell solve, accepted unchanged when it
+converges inside the declared bounds without leaving any expression's domain. Otherwise stage 2 runs a deterministic
+multi-start Powell in bound-respecting coordinates (exp/logistic maps of the DECLARED bounds); starts whose iterates
+leave an expression's domain are abandoned and counted. Distinct roots are classified exactly like scalar blocks:
+none -> `not_converged` (new status: the search found no solution, existence undecided), none admissible ->
+`invalid_domain`, several admissible -> `ambiguous`, one -> solved. No logarithm argument is clamped, no equation is
+changed, no ExprError is swallowed: a domain exit is a recorded solver event with its relation, point and message.
+Also guarded (crash paths only): a scalar bracket that straddles a hole in an expression's domain is skipped; a
+redundant equation undefined at the solution is a failed consistency check (conflict); an inequality undefined at
+the solution is `not_evaluable`. For the incident model the fixed solver finds both intersections of the laminar
+line and the Colebrook curve, (0.0619, 1033.9) and (591.4, 0.108), verifies they satisfy both equations, and rejects
+both by Colebrook's own validity condition (Re >= 4000): status `invalid_domain`.
+**Behaviour changes outside the crash path (deliberate, measured).** (a) a coupled root converged OUTSIDE declared
+bounds is no longer accepted (old code accepted it); (b) a coupled block on which Powell does not converge now gets
+the stage-2 search and is reported `not_converged` (no root found) or `invalid_domain` (roots found, none admissible)
+instead of `no_root`. Measured: (i) sweep over the 40 train-split tasks x every subset of <= 3 of the 30 usable
+fragments (181,000 solves, `scripts/solver_sweep.py --k 3 --split train`, old vs new checkout): 180,096 identical,
+336 former crashes -> `invalid_domain`, 568 `no_root` -> `not_converged`, 0 exceptions after the fix; (ii) committed
+E5 benchmark: 8 greedy-retrieval baseline episodes changed in an observation - 4 `beam_underdetermined`
+(`no_root` -> `not_converged`) and 4 `rocket_chamber_wall_stress` (held-out; `no_root` -> `invalid_domain`) - with
+identical outcomes, returns, step counts and scores; every constructor episode is identical; both committed training
+datasets (AGENT-V1, AGENT-V2) are byte-identical when rebuilt. `trajectories_e5.jsonl` is regenerated with the fixed
+code (two regenerations are byte-identical).
+**Malformed model actions (found in review).** ModelEnv raised `TypeError`/`ValueError` on 13 malformed payloads
+(list/dict `var` or `id`, non-numeric `value`); under the runner's handler these would have been mislabelled
+`infra_error`. They now take the transitions the environment already defines for an unknown name/id or a missing
+value (actions that did not raise behave exactly as before), are scored as MODEL outcomes, and count in
+`invalid_action_rate` through `well_formed()` (ModelEnv and ClaimEnv). Tested by an exhaustive junk-payload sweep.
+**Decision (evaluation).** `agent.batch_runner`: an exception from the deterministic core while starting or stepping
+an episode closes THAT episode as `outcome="infra_error"` (`return=None`, phase, step, action, error type, message,
+traceback tail); the rest of the batch proceeds unchanged (tested: identical episodes with and without the failure).
+Reference policies run under the same policy (`run_policy`). `eval_generations`: rates are computed over
+model-attributable episodes only; infra errors are counted in an `infra` column, listed by task in summary.md, and
+retried on the next run. Every suite reports attempted / scored / infra counts; rates use scored episodes
+only and are omitted (n/a in summary.md) when nothing was scoreable. On every resume, each saved episode is re-validated by replaying its recorded actions
+through the current environment (`batch_runner.replay`); episodes the current environment would judge differently
+are re-run, so one table never mixes two environment versions. The scoring policy is printed in summary.md.
+**Limits.** Multi-start is not a proof of non-existence (hence `not_converged`, never `no_root`, for coupled
+blocks); the start grid is fixed (<= 48 starts); ambiguity among coupled roots is detected only when stage 1 fails.
